els, and resembling a towering hill, or a mountain burnt, marshalling the Raksasa, said with a complacent countenance, “Like fire consuming insects, shall I today, wrought up with wroth, burn up one by one the bands of the flower of the monkeyforces.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.875,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-65-34"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6053035","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"120300678b1f9ee3177c37fdb02774d21a6f60006eab9ce1be7a9c09e54aa42e","compact":"660abe84df04c20a971f7b7f60987484315c3899b9504d151856e4872721ba9e","nasal":"009cd16c26e8f1567880f8bd003201ed7a865984f4b545ad4bdf8c4644f09916"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-53-36","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":53,"verse":36,"english":"And the gigantic huge-faced and fierce Kumbhakarņa, measuring an hundred bows in breadth and six hundred in altitude, having eyes resembling car-wheels, and resembling a towering hill, or a mountain burnt, marshalling the Raksasa, said with a complacent countenance, “Like fire consuming insects, shall I today, wrought up with wroth, burn up one by one the bands of the flower of the monkeyforces.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.833,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-65-34"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6053036","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e74c56b3ad18536c31c19e84c7361e1954ed6ac0d5c86d1bf95be3c33885065a","compact":"f0ec959fd8ed1e5f84ea5eab292b479e795fcf2aaa714261ffba9e1c672c66ba","nasal":"f0ec959fd8ed1e5f84ea5eab292b479e795fcf2aaa714261ffba9e1c672c66ba"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-53-37","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":53,"verse":37,"english":"But the wood-ranging monkeys have not transgressed anywise to the like of us. That species is the ornament of the gardens attached to our residences.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"metho